<section xml:id="sec-ch04-01-ArcLength" xmlns:xi="http://www.w3.org/2001/XInclude">

  <title>Arc Length</title>

  <note>
    <p>
      Given a curve
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>, we
      will derive a formula for calculating the length of <m>\vec{r}(t)</m>.
      First, we will talk about integrability.
    </p>
  </note>

  <definition>
    <title>Integrability of Curves</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given a curve
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>,
        then
        <md>
          \int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}(t) d t=\left\langle\int_{a}^{b} f(t) d t, \int_{a}^{b} g(t) d t, \int_{a}^{b} h(t) d t\right\rangle
        </md>
      </p>

      <p>
        Hence, <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> is integrable on <m>[a, b]</m> if and only
        if each of its components is integrable (continuous) on
        <m>[a, b]</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-01-ex-14-1">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Compute
        <m>\int_{0}^{1}\left\langle 3 t^{2}-2t, e^t+\cos(2\pi t)\right\rangle \, dt</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By the definition of integrability of curves, we integrate
        component-wise. For the first component:
        <md>
          \int_0^1 3t^2 - 2t\, dt = \left. t^3 - t^2 \right|_0^1 = (1 - 1) - (0 - 0) = 0
        </md>
        For the second component:
        <md>
          \int_0^1 e^t + \cos(2\pi t)\, dt = \left. e^t + \frac{\sin(2\pi t)}{2\pi} \right|_0^1 = \left( e + \frac{\sin(2\pi)}{2\pi} \right) - \left( e^0 + \frac{\sin(0)}{2\pi} \right) = e - 1
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          \int_{0}^{1}\left\langle 3 t^{2}-2t, e^t+\cos(2\pi t)\right\rangle \, dt = \langle 0, e-1 \rangle
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <p>
      Given
      <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle, \int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}(t) d t</m>
      is NOT computing the net area bounded by the curve and the
      <m>x y</m>-plane (there is a way to do this which we will talk about
      later).
    </p>

    <p>
      What <m>\int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}(t) d t</m> computes is a displacement
      vector.
    </p>
  </remark>

  <definition>
    <title>Displacement Vector</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Given an integrable curve <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(t), a \leq t \leq b</m>,
        then
        <md>
          \int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}^{\prime}(t) d t=\vec{r}(b)-\vec{r}(a)
        </md>
        gives a displacement vector with starting point at the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}(a)</m> and ending point at the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Moreover, <m>\left|\int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}^{\prime}(t) d t\right|</m>
        gives the displacement from the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(a)</m> to
        the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-01-displacement-vector">
    <caption>Two vectors, r of a and r of b, sharing a common starting point A, with endpoints C and B respectively, and the displacement vector r of b minus r of a, drawn connecting point C to point B.</caption>

    <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2. -->
    <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-01-displacement-vector" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
      <instructions>
        <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
        <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
      </instructions>
      <static>
        <image source="/images/2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2.png">
          <shortdescription>Two vectors, r of a and r of b, sharing a common starting point A, with endpoints C and B respectively, and the displacement vector r of b minus r of a, drawn connecting point C to point B.</shortdescription>
          <description>
            <p>Two vectors, <m>\vec{r}(a)</m> and <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>, sharing a common starting point <m>A</m>, with endpoints <m>C</m> and <m>B</m> respectively, and the displacement vector <m>\vec{r}(b)-\vec{r}(a)</m>, drawn connecting point <m>C</m> to point <m>B</m>.</p>
          </description>
        </image>
      </static>
    </interactive>
  </figure>

  <remark>
    <p>
      When we say the displacement from the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(a)</m>
      to the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>, in the above image, the
      distance between points B and C (NOT the distance along
      <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> ).
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-01-ex-14-2">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Below is the graph of
        <m>\vec r\left(t\right)=\langle t ,\ln\left(\cos\left(t-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\right)+1 \rangle, 0 \lt t \lt \pi</m>.
        You can see the displacement vector from the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> to the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)\right)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Compare this to travelling from the endpoint
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> to the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)\right)</m> along
        <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> colored in light blue.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-01-ex-14-2">
        <caption>The curve given by x equals t and y equals the natural log of the cosine of, t minus pi over 2, plus 1, for t strictly between 0 and pi, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2(1). -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-01-ex-14-2" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2-1.png">
              <shortdescription>The curve given by x equals t and y equals the natural log of the cosine of, t minus pi over 2, plus 1, for t strictly between 0 and pi, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The curve given by <m>x=t</m> and <m>y=\ln\left(\cos\left(t-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\right)+1</m>, for <m>0 \lt t \lt \pi</m>, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>
  </example>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-01-ex-14-3">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Suppose that a curve <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> satisfies
        <m>\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)=\langle 2 t, 1\rangle</m>. Find the
        displacement of a particle moving along <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> from
        <m>t=1</m> to <m>t=3</m>.
      </p>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        By the definition of the displacement vector, we integrate
        <m>\vec{r}'(t)</m> component-wise:
        <md>
          \int_1^3 \vec{r}'(t)\, dt = \int_1^3 \langle 2t, 1 \rangle\, dt = \left\langle \left. t^2 \right|_1^3, \left. t \right|_1^3 \right\rangle = \langle 9-1, 3-1 \rangle = \langle 8, 2 \rangle
        </md>
        The displacement of the particle is the magnitude of this
        displacement vector:
        <md>
          \left| \int_1^3 \vec{r}'(t)\, dt \right| = |\langle 8, 2 \rangle| = \sqrt{8^2 + 2^2} = \sqrt{68} = 2\sqrt{17}
        </md>
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <note>
    <p>
      Recall that integrals show up when we are summing up infinitely many
      things that are becoming infinitely small.
    </p>
  </note>

  <p>
    For example, given an integrable function
    <m>p(x), \int_{a}^{b} p(x) d x</m> calculates the net area bounded by
    <m>p(x)</m> and the <m>x</m>-axis on <m>[a, b]</m> by adding areas of
    rectangles that are becoming infinitely small.
  </p>

