  <p>
    As with area, as the number of line segments approaches infinity (their
    lengths become infinitely small), we will get a better approximation of
    the arc length.
  </p>

  <definition>
    <title>Length of a Curve (Arc length)</title>

    <statement>
      <p>
        Consider a curve
        <m>\vec{r}(t)=\langle f(t), g(t), h(t)\rangle, a \leq t \leq b</m>,
        where <m>f^{\prime}, g^{\prime}</m>, and <m>h^{\prime}</m> are
        continuous on <m>[a, b]</m> and the curve is traversed only once on
        <m>[a, b]</m>. Then the arclength of the curve between
        ( <m>f(a), g(a), h(a)</m> ) and ( <m>f(b), g(b), h(b)</m> ) is
        <md>
          L=\int_{a}^{b} \sqrt{f^{\prime}(t)^{2}+g^{\prime}(t)^{2}+h^{\prime}(t)^{2}} d t=\int_{a}^{b}\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t
        </md>
      </p>
    </statement>
  </definition>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-01-ex-14-4">
    <statement>
      <p>
        A glider is soaring upward along the helix
        <m>r(t)=\langle 0.98\cos (t), 0.98\sin (t) ,  \sqrt{1-0.98^2}t\rangle</m>.
        How long is the glider's path from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=2 \pi</m> ?
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-01-ex-14-4">
        <caption>The helix given by x equals 0.98 cosine of t, y equals 0.98 sine of t, z equals t times the square root of, 1 minus 0.98 squared, in xyz-space, with an arrow from the origin to a point on the curve.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to frame_00. -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-01-ex-14-4" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/frame_00.png">
              <shortdescription>The helix given by x equals 0.98 cosine of t, y equals 0.98 sine of t, z equals t times the square root of, 1 minus 0.98 squared, in xyz-space, with an arrow from the origin to a point on the curve.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The helix given by <m>x=0.98\cos(t)</m>, <m>y=0.98\sin(t)</m>, <m>z=t\sqrt{1-0.98^2}</m>, in <m>xyz</m>-space, with an arrow from the origin to a point on the curve.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        The length of the glider's path is the arc length of
        <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> from <m>t=0</m> to <m>t=2\pi</m>. First we
        compute the derivative and its magnitude:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{r}'(t) = \left\langle -0.98\sin (t),\ 0.98\cos (t),\ \sqrt{1-0.98^2} \right\rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>|\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{0.98^2\sin^2 (t) + 0.98^2\cos^2 (t) + \left( \sqrt{1-0.98^2} \right)^2}</mrow>
          <mrow>= \sqrt{0.98^2\left( \sin^2 (t) + \cos^2 (t) \right) + 1 - 0.98^2} = \sqrt{0.98^2 + 1 - 0.98^2} = \sqrt{1} = 1</mrow>
        </md>
        Hence
        <md>
          L = \int_0^{2\pi} |\vec{r}'(t)|\, dt = \int_0^{2\pi} 1\, dt = \left. t \right|_0^{2\pi} = 2\pi
        </md>
        The glider moves with constant speed <m>1</m>, so the length of its
        path equals the elapsed time <m>2\pi</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

  <remark>
    <title>Displacement vs Arclength</title>

    <p>
      It is important not to confuse displacement with arclength. Here we
      summarize both:
    </p>

    <p>
      <ul>
        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\left|\int_{a}^{b} \vec{r}^{\prime}(t) d t\right|</m> is
            computing the displacement (straight line distance) from the
            endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(a)</m> to the endpoint of
            <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>

        <li>
          <p>
            <m>\int_{a}^{b}\left|\vec{r}^{\prime}(t)\right| d t</m> is
            computing the arc length (distance travelled along
            <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> ) from the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(a)</m> to
            the endpoint of <m>\vec{r}(b)</m>.
          </p>
        </li>
      </ul>
    </p>
  </remark>

  <example xml:id="example-ch04-01-ex-14-5">
    <statement>
      <p>
        Below is the graph of
        <m>\vec r\left(t\right)=\langle t ,\ln\left(\cos\left(t-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\right)+1 \rangle, 0 \lt t \lt \pi</m>.
        Calculate the displacement vector from the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> to the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)\right)</m>.
      </p>

      <p>
        Compare this to the length of the curve from the endpoint
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}\right)</m> to the endpoint of
        <m>\vec{r}\left(\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)\right)</m>along
        <m>\vec{r}(t)</m> colored in light blue.
      </p>

      <figure xml:id="fig-ch04-01-ex-14-5">
        <caption>The curve given by x equals t and y equals the natural log of the cosine of, t minus pi over 2, plus 1, for t strictly between 0 and pi, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</caption>

        <!-- This interactive is PENDING, it should correspond to 2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2(1). -->
        <interactive xml:id="interactive-ch04-01-ex-14-5" width="85%" aspect="4:3" desmos="vqavop8m24">
          <instructions>
            <p>You can minimize the side bar by clicking on the arrow icon. You can save a copy and create your own version of the graph by clicking on the "Save" button in the upper right corner of the graph. You can open in a new page from here:
            <url href="https://www.desmos.com/calculator/vqavop8m24" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer">link to the graph</url></p>
          </instructions>
          <static>
            <image source="/images/2025_10_09_e25295bb40ebd09a2492g-2-1.png">
              <shortdescription>The curve given by x equals t and y equals the natural log of the cosine of, t minus pi over 2, plus 1, for t strictly between 0 and pi, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</shortdescription>
              <description>
                <p>The curve given by <m>x=t</m> and <m>y=\ln\left(\cos\left(t-\frac{\pi}{2}\right)\right)+1</m>, for <m>0 \lt t \lt \pi</m>, with the arc length and the straight-line displacement both shown between the same two points on the curve.</p>
              </description>
            </image>
          </static>
        </interactive>
      </figure>
    </statement>

    <solution>
      <p>
        First we find the two endpoints. At <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}</m>:
        <md>
          \vec{r}\left( \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right) = \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2},\ \ln\left( \cos (0) \right) + 1 \right\rangle = \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2},\ \ln (1) + 1 \right\rangle = \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2},\ 1 \right\rangle
        </md>
        At <m>t=\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)</m>, using
        <m>\cos\left( \arccos (1/e) \right) = \frac{1}{e}</m>:
        <md>
          \vec{r}\left( \tfrac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e) \right) = \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e),\ \ln\left( \tfrac{1}{e} \right) + 1 \right\rangle = \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e),\ 0 \right\rangle
        </md>
        The displacement vector from the first endpoint to the second is
        their difference:
        <md>
          \left\langle \tfrac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e) - \tfrac{\pi}{2},\ 0 - 1 \right\rangle = \left\langle \arccos (1/e),\ -1 \right\rangle
        </md>
        so the displacement (straight line distance) is
        <m>\sqrt{\arccos^2 (1/e) + 1} \approx 1.56</m>.
      </p>
    </solution>

    <solution>
      <p>
        For the length of the curve we differentiate, using the chain rule
        on the second component:
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>\vec{r}'(t) = \left\langle 1,\ -\tan\left( t - \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right) \right\rangle</mrow>
          <mrow>|\vec{r}'(t)| = \sqrt{1 + \tan^2\left( t - \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right)} = \sqrt{\sec^2\left( t - \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right)} = \sec\left( t - \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right)</mrow>
        </md>
        where we dropped the absolute value since <m>t-\frac{\pi}{2}</m>
        lies in <m>\left[ 0, \arccos (1/e) \right]</m>, which is inside
        <m>\left[ 0, \frac{\pi}{2} \right)</m> where the secant is positive.
        Hence
        <md alignment="gather">
          <mrow>L = \int_{\frac{\pi}{2}}^{\frac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e)} \sec\left( t - \tfrac{\pi}{2} \right) dt =</mrow>
          <mrow>u = t - \tfrac{\pi}{2}, \quad du = dt, \quad t = \tfrac{\pi}{2} \Rightarrow u = 0, \quad t = \tfrac{\pi}{2}+\arccos (1/e) \Rightarrow u = \arccos (1/e)</mrow>
          <mrow>\int_0^{\arccos (1/e)} \sec (u)\, du = \left. \ln\left| \sec (u) + \tan (u) \right| \right|_0^{\arccos (1/e)} = \ln\left( e + \sqrt{e^2-1} \right) - \ln (1) =</mrow>
          <mrow>\ln\left( e + \sqrt{e^2-1} \right)</mrow>
        </md>
        using <m>\sec\left( \arccos (1/e) \right) = e</m> and
        <m>\tan\left( \arccos (1/e) \right) = \sqrt{e^2-1}</m>. Numerically
        the arc length is <m>\ln\left( e + \sqrt{e^2-1} \right) \approx 1.66</m>,
        which is larger than the displacement <m>\approx 1.56</m>, as
        expected: the straight line is the shortest path between the two
        endpoints.
      </p>
    </solution>
  </example>

</section>
